          "source_quote": "οὗ τὴν μορφὴν τὸ ἀνθρώπου βαστάζει σῶμα, οὗ εἵνεκεν ὁ οὐρανὸς καὶ πάντες οἱ ἀστέρες ὑπέμειναν δουλεύειν",
          "english_quote": "whose form the human body bears and for whose sake heaven and all the stars, though superior in essence, submitted to serve the one inferior in essence because of the superior one’s form.",
          "issue": "The coordination makes God the antecedent of both occurrences of οὗ. That is defensible, but the second οὗ can instead refer back to the human being or human body just mentioned. This materially changes whose sake the heavenly bodies serve for. The existing note identifies the human recipient of service but does not disclose this separate antecedent ambiguity.",
